\DOC CASES_FIRST_DISJ_ASSUM_TAC

\TYPE {CASES_FIRST_DISJ_ASSUM_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Finds the first assumption whose conclusion is a disjunction and splits the
goal on it.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to a goal {A ?- t} having an assumption of the form
{u \/ v}, it removes that assumption and leaves the conclusion {t} unchanged,
whatever its shape, producing two subgoals in which {u} and respectively {v}
are appended at the end of the remaining assumption list:
{
        A, u \/ v ?- t
    ========================  CASES_FIRST_DISJ_ASSUM_TAC
     A, u ?- t    A, v ?- t
}
\noindent Thus a right-nested disjunction gives the left disjunct in one branch
and the remaining disjunction in the other, so repeated use peels off cases one
at a time.

\FAILURE
In the observed traces it fails, with a search-style failure, on goals none of
whose assumptions is a disjunction, even when the conclusion itself is a
disjunction; all successful cases had at least one disjunctive assumption.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this two-way split, never a closed or single
goal.

\ENDDOC
